State of the art automatic rifle used by the Lion Airbourne and Kaiser's personal detachment. Considered to be the pinnacle of Lion small-arms if you ignore the staggering production cost and small magazine. While the Kaiser insists that the weapon is the best, he didn't raise a fuss when high command invested in the L44 instead. Uses 7.92x57mm 10rnd/75rnd magazines.
^green;Higher BASE STATS than other assault rifles.^reset;
^green;Hold down "Alt Fire" to switch between Full Auto and Semi-Auto.^reset;
^red;NO ATTACHMENT SLOTS.^reset;

T100 was the only Japanese SMG that was produced in any significant numbers, adopted in 1940. Due to failures of previous experimental designs, Japanese stuck to European style of SMGs with side magazines, like the MP-34s that the Navy used successefuly in Shanghai skirmishes. 
T100/44 is a significantly simplified late-war variant, with an increased rate of fire.
Chambered for ^yellow;8x22mm Ammo.^reset;
^green;Hold down "Alt Fire" to switch between Full Auto and Semi-Auto.^reset;

The native tengu clans of this land already had some familiarity with basic firearms prior to the arrival of both the tengu and human outsiders. Many of these tengu soldiers would prefer to use a bandolier of magilocks to harass enemy forces over a bow due to the weapon's ease of use. While the bow would still see use in large scale battlefields due to being quick to reload, the magilock would excel in brief skirmishes.
^yellow;Magishot ammunition determines damage and magazine capacity.^reset;
