^yellow;Chapter 1: The Basics^reset;

^green;Be an artist.^reset;

Every good trap begins with the right inspiration. ^red;Do you like to hurt people? 
^reset;Think about your favorite ways to do that as you work through this guide. 

Let those inspirations flow into your design as you bring your traps to life- figuratively and literally!

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 1: The Basics^reset;

^green;Use the right bait.^reset;

Consistently attracting victims to your traps requires good bait. Think about which kinds of bait you enjoy and the kinds of visitors they can attract.

For example, bandits love pixels, but you can attract ^red;interesting monsters^reset; by using those same bandits as bait!

It's important to experiment to find the right bait that works for you.

^yellow;Chapter 1: The Basics^reset;

^green;You're in control, ^red;but nobody else needs to know that.^reset;

One of the biggest secrets to trap design is allowing your intruders to feel like they're in control.

Careful planning can lull interlopers into a false sense of security, which is perfect for luring them right to where you want them!

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 3: Chaos Management^reset;

On paper, it's easy to fall into the trap ^#888888;(if you'll excuse the pun)^reset; of assuming that increasing the complexity of your traps or adding more layers will always result in better success rates.

Unfortunately, this premise is only partially true. 

To get the best results, what you actually require is ^red;Chaos Management.^reset;

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 3: Chaos Management^reset;

So, what is Chaos Management?
It's easiest to think of this discipline as a workflow for creating mistakes. ^green;At their core, all traps rely on a victim making at least one mistake.^reset;

Making mistakes easy for your guests without exposing yourself to the same pitfalls ^#888888;(literally or figuratively)^reset; can make the difference between an adventurer escaping with your treasure or enriching your treasury!
^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 3: Chaos Management^reset;

The final step in creating mistakes is ^green;subverting expectations!^reset;

A platform that drops out from beneath your feet... A door that locks behind you... A mimic that looks like a treasure chest...

Each of these classic examples deceives intruders in different ways, but what they all have in common is the conversion of something familiar and safe into something deadly.

^yellow;Chapter 3: Chaos Management^reset;

The first step in creating mistakes is creating ^green;distractions!^reset;

From spotlights to spike pits, it's important that intruders always have something interesting or provocative to look at to keep their focus away from the traps intended to cripple them or finish them off.

^red;Monsters, ^yellow;shiny objects, ^reset;and ^green;baited chests^reset; are all effective distractions!
^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 3: Chaos Management^reset;

The second step in creating mistakes is creating ^green;syncopation!^reset;

If you're not familiar with this funny word, syncopation is when something occurs that's off-rhythm or off-beat. ^green;Most species struggle with arrythmic timings,^reset; and even the faintest patterns, like the soft pulses of a crystal lamp, can have a huge impact on the potency of a surprise attack.

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 4: Practical Applications^reset;

Now that you're familiar with the necessary ingredients for a good trap, here are some sample setups that you can adapt to fit your lair.

^green;Bait and Switch:^reset;
Place a visible chest next to a button or switch that opens an obvious door. Wire the switch to a boulder trap that rolls a rock through the door as it opens. Surprise!

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 4: Practical Applications^reset;

^green;All Grown Up:^reset;
Juvenile monsters are easy to find and capture. They also make great distractions for adventurers while an adult monster closes in on its prey.

^green;Pass the Sand, Please:^reset;
It sounds so simple that it could never work, but sometimes just dumping a lot of sand on intruders will kill them. Consider also buying a shop vacuum.

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 4: Practical Applications^reset;

^green;Burning Oil:^reset;
Place enough oil to hide some landmines. If you add just the right amount, you can hide the trap without smothering the resultant flames!

^green;Spike Trap With Benefits:^reset;
Use trapdoors to drop your victim into a spike pit full of slime with an obvious exit. Mount a flamethrower there for an easy barbecue!

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 4: Practical Applications^reset;

^green;Just Add Scorpions:^reset;
Spruce up a pit or hallway with biting, stinging friends! Scorpions are a great way to force your victims away from sure footing and into other traps.

^green;The Reward Is Pain:^reset;
Wire up an elaborate switch puzzle next to a treasure chest that drops visitors into a pit. Make those pesky adventurers earn their suffering!

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 4: Practical Applications^reset;

^green;Other Fun Ideas:^reset;
Whether your palace needs defense from bandits or your lair needs an answer to roving treasure hunters, there's always room to get creative with new trap components. Here are a few more ideas to experiment with:
- ^yellow;Poisonous gases^reset;
- ^yellow;Tripwires and explosives^reset;
- ^yellow;Spinning sawblades^reset;
- ^yellow;Laser crystals^reset;
- ^yellow;Mimic chests^reset;
- ^yellow;Baby Shark 10 Hours (4K HD 60FPS)^reset;

^yellow;Chapter 5: Final Thoughts^reset;

With all of the information that's been covered so far in mind, it's important to step back and put the craft of trapping a lair in perspective.

Ideally, your traps will catch a victim off guard, and usually that's enough. Sometimes, however, traps don't finish the job.

^cyan;Always have a backup plan.^reset;

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 5: Final Thoughts^reset;

^green;If you're out of options, ask for help.^reset;

Veteran adventurers survive traps, but for as nimble as they might be, ^red;they're not usually terribly clever.^reset; Simply asking them to go fight monsters on your behalf is often enough incentive for adventurers!

^#888888;Being cute helps a lot here, but keep some pixels ready in case you need to use a bribe instead.^reset;
^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 5: Final Thoughts^reset;

^green;In a universe full of treasure to collect, there's always room for a few more deadly surprises.^reset;

Hopefully this guide helps your lair become a haven for you and a beacon for prosperity!

^red;...Delivered to you one dead adventurer at a time.

^yellow;Chapter 5: Final Thoughts^reset;

^green;Let the door hit them on the way out.^reset;

While it's technically riskier, another approach to dealing with especially troublesome adventurers is to simply let them load up on treasure before trying to actually kill them.

Placing traps along the exit route to target intruders who have their hands full works great for small parties, but lacks staying power against raids.
^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 5: Final Thoughts^reset;

^green;Sometimes you just have to kill them yourself.^reset;

If interlopers have made it all the way to your personal chambers, thwarted your traps, and dispatched of your minions, you may occasionally have to get your hands dirty.

Most adventurers are gullible, though, ^cyan;so just stab them in the back if you can get away with it.^reset;
^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;Chapter 5: Final Thoughts^reset;

^green;There's always another lair.^reset;

If all else fails, ^cyan;it's better to relocate to a new dungeon than it is to be slain at the end of one.^reset;

You can always occupy another crypt, erect another wizard tower, corrupt another temple from within, etc- and the change of scenery can be nice, too. Variety in life is good!

^#8888CC;>>

^yellow;DISCUSSION^reset;

Like Experiment #65, this experiment shows significant potential in the production of bioweapons. Future research similarly may attempt to work around this issue.

^cyan;L^reset;&^green;P^reset;

^yellow;DISCUSSION^reset;

These results prove to exceed expectations, although its tendency to root itself into the ground makes it too difficult to deploy efficiently in its current state. Further research in this area may attempt to get around this issue.

